{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794229824,"nonce":"7dv55GcyjxCs4rQkZDWaQlSISso=","title":"An id a DivRef assigns while binding renames every descendant's stable id","message":"`DivRef::new()` gives the bound element an id when it has none, in\n`bind_to_node`, during the build. Stable ids are derived from element ids\n(`mint_stable_ids_walk` reads `element_registry.get_id(child)`), and the id\nlands after the first mint. The next mint (any region change: a `for_each`\nrow, a `show` branch) derives different stable ids for the element and every\ndescendant. `sweep_stale_handlers` then drops their handlers, which were\nregistered under the old ids, and nothing registers them again. Every widget\nunder the bound element stops responding to clicks; signals, bindings and\nthe render loop keep working.\n\nFound through cn's resizable group, which bound a `DivRef` to each panel\nwrapper to read its laid-out size: in the russl toy every widget inside a\npanel (tabs, inspector controls, nested resize handles) stopped responding\nafter the first tab switch. The handle between top-level panels and the\nstatus bar, outside the wrappers, kept working.\n\ncn now gives each wrapper an explicit id at build time (`{key}__panel_{i}`),\nwith a regression test\n`a_panel_keeps_its_handlers_when_rows_change_elsewhere`. The underlying\nhazard remains for any `.bind(\u0026DivRef::new())` on an element without an id.\nOptions: assign the id before the element is minted, leave auto-assigned ids\nout of stable id derivation, or re-register handlers under re-minted ids.","files":null}]}